WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.253 --> 00:00:02.170
We lost our tail rotor!

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:06.600
That's the Hollywood part.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.248
I've seen helicopters crash on
tarmacs and different things,

00:00:10.248 --> 00:00:11.558
and I haven't seen one explode.

00:00:11.558 --> 00:00:13.200
Hey, my name is Dakata Brodie.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.055
I'm a 21-year military veteran.

00:00:15.055 --> 00:00:17.490
I served 13 years in the US Coast Guard

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.080
as a search-and-rescue-helicopter pilot

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.670
and eight years in the Army

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.070
as a UH-60 Black Hawk helicopter pilot.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.210
Today we're going to look at

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.389
helicopter rescue scenes in movies

00:00:26.389 --> 00:00:28.113
and judge how real they are.

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:33.794
All right. Tuck them in, boys.

00:00:33.794 --> 00:00:35.430
For the Coast Guard, primarily,

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.980
we face offshore and going off shores

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.690
to effect most of our rescues.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.490
However, the Coast Guard does train

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.830
for vertical-surface rescues,

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.240
which is what you're seeing right here.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.520
Cliffside, cliff rescues,
and everything else.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.679
Just stay perfectly still. I
have to stabilize the car, OK?

00:00:49.679 --> 00:00:50.760
Hurry!

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:51.960
Rescuer: Car secured.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.370
All right. If that is the hoist hook,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.220
you are in what you call
a committed situation.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.924
Every helo pilot would be
shaken at this point right now

00:01:05.924 --> 00:01:08.652
knowing that the hoist hook
is attached to a vehicle

00:01:08.652 --> 00:01:09.540
that could fall off.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.156
Not happening. We're not
connecting up to any vehicle.

00:01:12.156 --> 00:01:14.640
Joby, I'm coming down.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.840
Harrison, you're flying.

00:01:18.196 --> 00:01:20.580
So, a pilot going to the back seat?

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.770
First of all, you know how
much stuff you can knock off?

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.900
You could literally turn
off both of your engines.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.720
The fact that your vest
and everything could snag

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.040
and you could flip things off.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.070
We actually had a person
turn with their NVGs

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.330
sitting on top of their head

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.430
and actually hit one of the engines

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.320
and knock an engine offline with that.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.235
So, yeah, it's very possible

00:01:39.235 --> 00:01:41.396
to actually turn off
something inadvertently

00:01:41.396 --> 00:01:44.580
just climbing and trying to
get to the back of the cabin.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:45.730
Pilot: Out the door.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.985
Natalie, don't you worry. I'm
going to get you out of here!

00:01:49.985 --> 00:01:52.560
If I am outside the helicopter,

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.150
we have a real bad problem.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.673
As a pilot, I am never outside.

00:01:57.778 --> 00:01:59.358
I got you!

00:01:59.358 --> 00:02:00.317
Cut it!

00:02:00.317 --> 00:02:01.524
Joby, swing!

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.450
They were trying to show, like,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.730
the hoist was holding the truck up, right?

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.950
The hoist that you have
on there about 600 pounds.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:13.246
So it's not your primary
means of holding up anything.

00:02:13.246 --> 00:02:16.748
At that point that it was
smoking, it was straining so hard.

00:02:16.748 --> 00:02:19.230
Now, there is a squib that
you could flip the switch

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.457
to actually cut and
shear that hoist cable,

00:02:22.457 --> 00:02:24.600
which is what they were
doing at that point.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.002
So somehow they sheared the one cable,

00:02:28.002 --> 00:02:31.890
and The Rock and the other
person was on a different cable.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.180
I don't know what that is.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.785
I'll say that our helicopter has one cable

00:02:35.785 --> 00:02:36.810
that we're going to operate with.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:37.980
Once that cable gets sheared,

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.510
everything is going down with it.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.488
It's not going to happen
on so many different levels

00:02:41.488 --> 00:02:42.330
on this clip.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.090
This right here is a zero.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.215
Pilot: Where are they?

00:02:45.215 --> 00:02:46.710
Tanker's on the way.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.960
Air Force rescue 231.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.330
I don't know why nobody wears NVGs.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.367
Everybody has NVGs on,
night-vision goggles on,

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:56.492
in the helicopters. Nobody wears them.

00:02:56.492 --> 00:02:58.785
If you have the option of having them on,

00:02:58.785 --> 00:03:00.035
yeah, you're going to have them on.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.320
The fuel probe that you just saw,

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.315
that's not a standard piece
of equipment on helicopters.

00:03:10.315 --> 00:03:11.790
There's just certain helicopters

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.682
that are either doing
some long-range rescues.

00:03:14.682 --> 00:03:16.507
Most helicopters do not have that.

00:03:23.087 --> 00:03:23.987
Pilot: No!

00:03:23.987 --> 00:03:27.686
Your biggest thing at
that point is if you miss,

00:03:27.686 --> 00:03:30.385
where's that tube that's
going that's flowing fuel,

00:03:30.385 --> 00:03:32.267
if you're going to cut that
thing with the main rotor,

00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:34.733
you're talking about damage
to the main rotor system.

00:03:34.733 --> 00:03:36.851
You'd be in a bad spot at that point.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:45.210
Looking right now at a lightning strike.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:48.030
Knock on wood and thank God
I've never had that happen.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:50.400
You risk frying a lot of electronics,

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.320
engine-control systems that are on there.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.690
You're hoping that your
engines are staying online.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:57.150
You would probably lose
a lot of electronic

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.821
AC and DC systems inside the helicopter,

00:03:59.821 --> 00:04:01.702
but you should be able to keep flying

00:04:01.702 --> 00:04:04.994
unless you got hit in the
tail rotor or something.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.150
Every year, the Coast Guard has to do

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.560
what's called dunker training.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.440
So we get flipped over
in a chair in a pool,

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.240
which simulates having to ditch.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.123
You're rolled upside down.

00:04:19.123 --> 00:04:21.660
You have to do multiple different egresses

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.423
to show that you have the proficiency

00:04:23.423 --> 00:04:25.650
to be able to get out of
a submerged helicopter.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.822
Every single year, and
then every six years,

00:04:27.822 --> 00:04:29.280
unless they've changed
it, but every six years

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.776
you would go to actual school,

00:04:30.776 --> 00:04:32.789
where you go through the
whole mock-up simulator

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:34.740
of a device going in and rolling over.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.338
So this is a part of the business.

00:04:42.401 --> 00:04:44.640
He's got SEAs bottle or HEEDs bottle,

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:49.440
which gives you about two to
three minutes at normal breath.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.890
In that mode right there, you
have about 10 to 15 seconds

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.330
if they're breathing the
way I think they are.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.610
But the pure shock of going in

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.074
and the water temperature itself

00:04:58.074 --> 00:04:59.580
is going to take the breath out of you.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.560
But I do like the realistic aspect

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.780
of seeing somebody grab that SEAs bottle

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.072
and put that thing in their mouth.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.660
Now, for everybody that flies,

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.220
we have emergency jettison handles.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.080
So the normal exits are no longer

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.520
going to get you out of the door.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.220
It's too much pressure that's
on that door at that point.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:22.410
So you're literally hitting
your emergency jettison

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.845
like it's blowing cartridges,
and the door pops off.

00:05:24.845 --> 00:05:27.567
It just is lifting up the
actual door-hinge pins

00:05:27.567 --> 00:05:29.606
out of the way so you can create

00:05:29.606 --> 00:05:31.500
that full opening at that point.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.740
Anybody's going for that,
yeah, you're going to die.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.632
I'd give this one probably about a six.

00:05:36.632 --> 00:05:37.804
You can lower me down.

00:05:37.804 --> 00:05:38.763
I'll get the two men.

00:05:38.763 --> 00:05:41.278
You return and pick us up on my signal.

00:05:42.283 --> 00:05:45.120
The theatrics on there is amazing.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.590
They had no business being that close

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.850
to a rig that's exploding.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:50.970
So, we've done multiple
rescues in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.170
I was stationed at before.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.790
So, flying out to rigs,

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.920
rigs typically have burn-offs happening,

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.330
but there's always either gases

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:58.650
or stuff like that that's coming off.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:02.533
So the rigs all have more of
escape-type pods and things

00:06:02.533 --> 00:06:04.972
for the rig workers to be able to get out

00:06:04.972 --> 00:06:06.720
and be able to get off of the rigs,

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.210
and then we would effect the
rescue from the open water.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.503
To try to go into there again,
we're no good to anybody,

00:06:12.503 --> 00:06:15.042
and it's so much risk
of that flying debris,

00:06:15.042 --> 00:06:17.880
shrapnel going through
the rotor-blade system.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.320
You're just now a victim.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.236
You're not going to put yourself

00:06:20.236 --> 00:06:22.670
or the crew in that situation.

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:23.995
Rescuer: Count five.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:30.240
Five.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.970
Any standard deployment
of the rescue swimmer,

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:34.410
they're just going down.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.417
At that point, you just
call it a direct deployment

00:06:36.417 --> 00:06:37.320
of the rescue swimmer.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.300
Especially when you're deploying somebody

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.700
either to a boat that's underway,

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:42.750
you'll have them down,

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.910
you'll have them just off
the water or just above.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.075
But to say that you were
about to send somebody

00:06:47.075 --> 00:06:51.116
through a flame, a blowtorch in essence,

00:06:51.116 --> 00:06:52.024
not gonna happen.

00:06:53.226 --> 00:06:54.411
Rescuer: One!

00:07:00.533 --> 00:07:02.220
When somebody is on the cable,

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.480
the hook is actually locked
just to prevent any mishap

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.500
to where it could inadvertently come open.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.950
For somebody to pirouette

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:12.617
and unhook themself off of the cable

00:07:12.617 --> 00:07:14.670
at the same time, that's pretty amazing.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:16.439
Nobody would be that crazy

00:07:16.439 --> 00:07:19.650
to even attempt to put
somebody in that situation.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:20.940
The rescue swimmer would come up

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.839
and slap everybody in the helicopter

00:07:22.839 --> 00:07:24.720
for even putting them down
in something like that.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.603
So absolutely not.

00:07:26.603 --> 00:07:29.402
It's completely
unrealistic. That's a zero.

00:07:33.549 --> 00:07:34.837
Pilot: Swimmers away!

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.202
When he jumps from the helicopter,

00:07:40.202 --> 00:07:43.344
that's a free-fall deployment
of the rescue swimmer.

00:07:43.344 --> 00:07:45.030
That is reserved for daytime only,

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:48.358
so we would've only hoisted
the rescue swimmer down

00:07:48.358 --> 00:07:51.393
at that point, not let him
jump from the helicopter.

00:07:51.393 --> 00:07:55.008
They're seeing, like, 20-foot
swells or something like that.

00:07:55.008 --> 00:07:56.828
That means the helicopter is hovering

00:07:56.828 --> 00:07:58.650
at least 30 to 40 feet.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:02.580
You misjudge that swell, that's
a four- or five-story drop.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:05.100
That water turns into a solid real quick,

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:07.033
especially if that was cold temperatures.

00:08:08.336 --> 00:08:10.125
I have the ready-for-pickup signal.

00:08:10.125 --> 00:08:12.486
Basket is going out of the cabin door.

00:08:13.403 --> 00:08:14.970
Basket is in the water.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:16.680
It's a standard rescue basket.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.255
He's calling for the basket,
has the ready-for-pickup sign.

00:08:19.255 --> 00:08:20.490
That's exactly what you would hear,

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:21.973
because we're standing by,

00:08:21.973 --> 00:08:24.090
keeping our light in the
direction of the swimmer,

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:26.100
kind of making sure we
always have sight on them.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:27.173
And as they're getting ready,

00:08:27.173 --> 00:08:28.560
you're going to try to place the device

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.618
really within about
five to 10 feet of them

00:08:30.618 --> 00:08:32.076
once they're calling for it.

00:08:32.076 --> 00:08:34.230
So very, very real.

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:35.520
Pilot: How we looking on fuel there?

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:37.356
Crew: Bingo, we're at bingo. Gotta go.

00:08:37.356 --> 00:08:39.300
We're out of time. We gotta go, Carl.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.300
That "bingo" fuel, that's a real term.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.811
It just means if you leave now,

00:08:44.811 --> 00:08:46.680
you will land with a fuel reserve

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:48.105
when you get back at your station.

00:08:48.105 --> 00:08:49.920
Anything that's less than there,

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.260
you can be at risk of either not making it

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.870
to your destination or
airport or flaming out.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.753
It's always a race against
how much fuel you have.

00:08:59.921 --> 00:09:02.654
Go, go! Behind you!

00:09:02.654 --> 00:09:04.107
Hurry!

00:09:04.107 --> 00:09:05.725
When you're seeing the winds,

00:09:05.725 --> 00:09:07.800
that rotor wash, it's beating down on you,

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:09.390
it's a mini hurricane underneath it.

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:12.270
There is no communicating
with the swimmer in the water

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.461
at that point in time.
All hand-arm signals.

00:09:14.461 --> 00:09:15.990
That would be insane to think

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.490
that you're going to hear a person,

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.460
a swimmer in the water
underneath a spinning helicopter.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:21.731
Not gonna happen on there.

00:09:21.731 --> 00:09:23.247
Holy s---!

00:09:25.321 --> 00:09:27.180
We lost our tail rotor!

00:09:30.338 --> 00:09:33.222
The waves out there, that's
in the Arctic, kind of,

00:09:33.222 --> 00:09:35.310
where I believe that
scene was taking place.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:38.430
20-, 30-foot seas, and even
having rogue waves can happen,

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.353
but you try to be above that.

00:09:40.353 --> 00:09:42.574
The explosion, that's the Hollywood part.

00:09:42.574 --> 00:09:46.269
I've seen helicopters crash on
tarmacs and different things,

00:09:46.269 --> 00:09:47.700
and I haven't seen one explode.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.476
Even in the Army, I saw one take a SA-7

00:09:50.476 --> 00:09:51.570
and hit the engine out.

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:53.550
That was the only one
I saw that was on fire.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:54.924
I would give it a seven.

00:09:54.924 --> 00:09:56.615
It's the Coast Guard.

00:09:56.615 --> 00:09:58.350
They must have picked
up the distress call.

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:00.550
Pilot: Swimmers ready. Deploy swimmers.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.390
So far right now, you're
seeing pretty much

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:08.451
a legitimate helicopter rescue.

00:10:08.451 --> 00:10:11.970
It's daytime. You can free-fall
the swimmer in daytime.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:13.410
Their altitude was good,

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:15.510
where the swimmer could safely go out.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.550
The swimmer's going to have
their goggles, everything down.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.830
They're not going to
free-fall with their gear up.

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:21.720
They go in the water, they're
going to lose all their gear.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:22.590
So they're going down.

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:24.279
They're going to cover
their mask and everything

00:10:24.279 --> 00:10:25.129
as they're going in.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.850
The device going down and being placed

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:32.370
about five feet from them in the water,

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:35.159
that's just textbook Coast Guard

00:10:35.159 --> 00:10:36.926
rescue-swimmer operations on there.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.010
When you have baywatch,

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:46.440
that's really the LA
County's fire department.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:50.160
They're aka baywatch here
in the Los Angeles area.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:51.510
We hoist with them routinely.

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:52.620
The rescue swimmer could easily

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:54.090
just bring them over to the vessel.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:56.430
The fire department, they're
trained paramedics as well.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:58.925
Primarily, you're going
to try to put people in

00:10:58.925 --> 00:11:02.490
with the least-risky rescue method,

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:04.380
which would be to put them on the boat,

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:06.465
unless they had sustained something else

00:11:06.465 --> 00:11:08.340
that they needed immediate transport,

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:09.450
bring them in a helicopter

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:11.310
and transport them to
the nearest hospital.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:14.220
But the rescue in itself,
that's pretty textbook

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.723
for what you could see

00:11:15.723 --> 00:11:18.210
if you're going to free-fall
the rescue swimmer, yes.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:21.360
It's a nine, just because
of how the rescue swimmer

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:24.193
had his goggles and
stuff on top of his head.

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:31.140
I love the precision flying of that.

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:34.830
This is the MH-68 or AW109, the Stingray.

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.690
Really what you'll call for HITRON,

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.360
which is our helicopters
tactics interdiction.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:41.737
It's a specialized unit that we have that

00:11:41.737 --> 00:11:45.282
primarily their focus is
interdicting either drug smugglers

00:11:45.282 --> 00:11:47.599
or anything else like that
in international waters.

00:11:47.599 --> 00:11:49.907
Mostly dealing with drug smuggling,

00:11:49.907 --> 00:11:51.240
what you'll call a go-fast.

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:52.680
It'll typically outrun a boat.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:54.167
Your normal cruise is somewhere around

00:11:54.167 --> 00:11:56.670
the 120 to 130, 130 knots,

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:59.512
if you really got a good
tailwind and stuff like that.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:03.270
Watch out, he's got a weapon on board.

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:04.353
Bail out! Bail out!

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:08.400
I've never been at HITRON.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:12.240
I did fly airborne use of
force with the Coast Guard.

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:13.747
A lot of times what you're trying to do is

00:12:13.747 --> 00:12:15.360
we're going to try to stay outside

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.880
of what they have on board of there

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:20.070
and make sure we can at least maximize

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:21.900
the use of our weapons systems.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.700
Coast Guard, take them down.

00:12:28.169 --> 00:12:30.000
They went for the kill shots right there.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.564
So, normally they're going to probably

00:12:31.564 --> 00:12:33.660
put some stitches in the water.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:35.507
They say "stitches in the
water" because when you see it,

00:12:35.507 --> 00:12:38.490
it looks like stitches from
the machine gun going out.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:41.112
That's kind of just to escalate the level

00:12:41.112 --> 00:12:43.350
of the use of force at that point

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:45.360
before they try to shoot the engines out

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:49.860
and really are more so trying
to just disable the vessel.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:51.376
That's primarily what they're doing, yeah,

00:12:51.376 --> 00:12:52.860
is disabling the vessel.

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:54.763
So I'd give them about a seven.

00:12:59.505 --> 00:13:02.130
At any point in time you're committed,

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:03.900
you have something that's attached

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.767
to something that can pull you down,

00:13:05.767 --> 00:13:08.280
that's just the worst situation
for a helicopter, right?

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:12.240
So you're just at the mercy
of being on that pivot point

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:14.015
and just completely helpless.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.205
Wet, slippery hand conditions,

00:13:23.205 --> 00:13:25.550
trying to hold on to a
ladder with adrenaline

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:26.880
and the amount of rotor wash.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:28.020
It's a mini hurricane down there.

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.180
So you're holding on
with quite a bit of grip.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.010
So I'm pretty sure you're almost probably

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:34.252
bear-hugging the ladder at any point

00:13:34.252 --> 00:13:35.854
just to try to maintain your grip.

00:13:35.854 --> 00:13:37.491
We're going to stall!

00:13:37.491 --> 00:13:38.768
Cut it now!

00:13:38.768 --> 00:13:39.601
Mercenary: We have a job!

00:13:39.601 --> 00:13:42.510
Pilot: We're going to
die here! I said cut it!

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:44.960
They were actually doing
well talking as a crew

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:46.410
for saying get rid of it.

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:47.460
That's a tough call.

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:50.040
It sounds easy to watch
the movie and say that,

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.280
but that is a really
hard call to say shear.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:54.933
Even when we're getting ready to start

00:13:54.933 --> 00:13:57.480
any hoist and rescues,
like, "Hey, if this happens,

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:00.240
you'll say 'shear' three
times, safety power system

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.550
or the flight mech, you
race for that shear button."

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:05.391
It was an eight for how dynamic
that environment would be.

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:14.160
The stretcher. We call it a litter.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.420
Anytime we're going to use a litter,

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:18.240
we're going to use a trail
line to help stabilize,

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:21.848
because that flat plane
surface that's underneath

00:14:21.848 --> 00:14:24.093
with all the rotor wash that's down below

00:14:24.093 --> 00:14:25.458
will really have a good propensity

00:14:25.458 --> 00:14:27.483
to have somebody start
spinning really quick.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:37.770
I'm not sure what was causing
the pilot to struggle so much.

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:41.130
He's got the whole ship to
look at as far as in reference.

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:42.695
Power really wasn't an issue.

00:14:42.695 --> 00:14:45.118
It almost looked like they
were making the hoist cable

00:14:45.118 --> 00:14:48.660
feel like it was somehow taking
power away from the engines.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:49.515
I don't know.

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:57.660
Having a fouled cable or watching

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:01.230
a cable actually birdcage,
that part is real.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.840
That can happen, so it could
go in there and not be.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:06.040
You can't let out some
more cable at that point.

00:15:16.069 --> 00:15:18.990
I don't understand why the
pilot was struggling so bad.

00:15:18.990 --> 00:15:19.920
I'm not sure.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:22.031
At this point, what's got a hold of them

00:15:22.031 --> 00:15:24.030
is some good bait that's
clearly under there

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:25.590
for whatever is eating on them.

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:28.651
So there's no need to take
the whole helicopter down.

00:15:28.651 --> 00:15:29.912
Shear, shear, shear.

00:15:29.912 --> 00:15:31.853
We'll have to preserve the
lives of the rest of the crew.

00:15:31.853 --> 00:15:33.180
There's nothing else you could do.

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:35.180
This is more of a three.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:44.970
Please don't shoot the
flare at the helicopter.

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:47.775
We see the flare in
the distance. Trust us.

00:15:47.775 --> 00:15:50.042
If the flare gets on the raft,
it'll start eating through.

00:15:50.042 --> 00:15:51.690
It's, like, a phosphor material,

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:54.022
so you're shooting hot phosphorus

00:15:54.022 --> 00:15:55.746
at the thing that's trying to rescue you.

00:15:55.746 --> 00:15:57.240
You're trying to take us down with you

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:58.380
at that point in time.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:01.170
The flare is meant to be
seen, not to be caught.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:03.000
We just want to see the flare go up

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:04.620
and then find the point of origin

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:06.236
so we can come and effect the rescue.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.003
A lot of times, if we're going out,

00:16:14.003 --> 00:16:15.957
it's more likely just one helicopter.

00:16:15.957 --> 00:16:17.130
I'm not saying you can't have two.

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:19.170
If it's a longshore rescue as well,

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:21.495
there's a fixed wing asset overhead

00:16:21.495 --> 00:16:22.770
that will probably go out and do

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:25.200
really good radar sweeps
inside of the area

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:27.000
and try to help us. And
they'll also be able

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:30.488
to drop and deliver any
type of rescue materials.

00:16:30.488 --> 00:16:32.326
Certain helicopters do have radar systems,

00:16:32.326 --> 00:16:34.200
so we're trying to either
see a blip on the radar,

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.930
or we're trying to map the
shoreline when we're out there

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:38.630
in really poor weather conditions.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:44.910
Helicopters have a few different lights.

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:46.830
So, each pilot have their own searchlight,

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:48.600
and then a lot of times you'll operate

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:49.915
with what's called a Nightsun.

00:16:49.915 --> 00:16:52.740
So it's just a really powerful searchlight

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:54.630
towards the middle or
the back of the cabin.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:58.591
